## Azure Policy selection

| | |
|---|---|
| Assignments created | {{FACTORY-RAW:computed.policyAssignmentsCreated}} of {{FACTORY-RAW:computed.policyAssignmentsTotal}} |
| Changed from the library baseline | {{FACTORY-RAW:computed.policyAssignmentsChanged}} |

The set comes from the Azure Landing Zones library at the ref this factory
pins; the selection was made in the wizard's Policies step and is recorded in
`lz-config.json` under `governance.policySelection`. An assignment nobody
changed is created exactly as the library declares it, so a library upgrade
adds its new policies rather than being silently narrowed by an older answer
record.

The changes themselves are in
`terraform/live/global/terraform.auto.tfvars`, as `policy_assignment_changes`.
Two states are different and worth keeping apart: `creation_enabled = false`
means the assignment is never created, while `enforcement_mode =
"DoNotEnforce"` means it is created and reports compliance without acting.

Do not hand-edit those files to change a policy. The
`policy-diff-guardrails.yml` check rejects a downgrade that is not accompanied
by a re-exported answer record, and the next render would undo it regardless.
Re-run the wizard and regenerate.

## Management groups

#{{IF computed.hasRenamedGroups}}
This estate uses its own names for the Azure Landing Zones management groups.
They are declared in
`terraform/live/global/lib/architecture_definitions/{{FACTORY-RAW:computed.architectureName}}.alz_architecture_definition.json`,
which the `alz` provider loads **alongside** the pinned library rather than
instead of it: every archetype, policy definition and policy assignment still
comes from `{{FACTORY-RAW:computed.alzLibraryPath}}@{{FACTORY-RAW:computed.alzLibraryRef}}`,
and the local file says only what the groups are called.

Only the names are this estate's. Every group the library defines is still
created, and the parent edges and the archetype each group carries are the
library's, deliberately: an archetype is attached to a management group by the
architecture definition, so moving a group under a different parent changes
which policy set governs everything beneath it.
#{{ELSEIF computed.hasTrimmedGroups}}
This estate uses the library's own names for its management groups, but **not
all of the library's groups**. The CAF-minimal hierarchy is Platform and
Landing Zones only: the {{FACTORY-RAW:computed.droppedGroupNames}} groups are
**not created**.

The trimmed list is declared in
`terraform/live/global/lib/architecture_definitions/{{FACTORY-RAW:computed.architectureName}}.alz_architecture_definition.json`,
which the `alz` provider loads **alongside** the pinned library rather than
instead of it: every archetype, policy definition and policy assignment still
comes from `{{FACTORY-RAW:computed.alzLibraryPath}}@{{FACTORY-RAW:computed.alzLibraryRef}}`.

Nothing is renamed, and nothing is re-parented. The two groups omitted are
direct children of the root with no children of their own, so every remaining
group keeps the parent and the archetype the library gives it. A subscription
that would have been placed in one of the omitted groups has nowhere to go —
which is why the render refuses a sandbox subscription under this strategy
rather than placing it somewhere it was not meant to be.
#{{ELSE}}
This estate uses the management groups the pinned Azure Landing Zones library
defines, with the library's own names. The hierarchy comes from
`{{FACTORY-RAW:computed.alzLibraryPath}}@{{FACTORY-RAW:computed.alzLibraryRef}}`.
#{{ENDIF}}

**Management-group IDs are immutable in Azure.** Changing one after the first
apply means creating a new group and moving every subscription and assignment
into it — this is not a decision a later release corrects.

Workload subscriptions are placed in `{{FACTORY-RAW:computed.workloadManagementGroupId}}`.
That choice does not change how many policy assignments they inherit: the
`online` group carries none of its own and inherits the landing-zones set plus
the root's either way. What it decides is whether Corp and Online can be told
apart later without moving the subscription, since Corp adds five deny-style
assignments that Online does not.

## Control hierarchy

1. Tenant and management groups define organizational scope.
2. Subscription placement establishes platform and workload boundaries.
3. Azure Policy evaluates location, tagging, diagnostics, encryption, network,
   and service-specific controls.
4. Repository policy checks reject IaC changes that weaken the baseline.
5. Protected environments authorize deployment.
6. Continuous compliance and drift checks detect out-of-band changes.

#{{IF governance.policyBaseline.enforcementMode == 'audit'}}
> Policy is configured in audit mode. The assignments that would block a
> deployment — the Deny and DenyAction ones — are created with
> `enforcement_mode = "DoNotEnforce"`, so non-compliance is still reported and
> nothing is refused. This is what Azure Landing Zones recommends for softening
> a deny: change the enforcement mode, not the policy's effect.
>
> Everything else keeps the library's own enforcement, deliberately.
> `DoNotEnforce` suspends the effect **during resource creation and update**,
> so a DeployIfNotExists or Modify assignment stops deploying as resources
> appear or change. Remediation is not lost — remediation tasks can still be
> run by hand against a DoNotEnforce assignment — but the estate no longer
> converges on its own, so Defender configuration, the Azure Monitor Agent,
> diagnostic settings and private-DNS registration would drift until someone
> remediates them deliberately.
>
> Promotion to deny requires a clean compliance report, approved exemptions,
> and a tested rollback path. It is a wizard answer, not a hand edit.
#{{ELSE}}
Policy enforcement is blocking: every assignment keeps the enforcement mode
the library declares for it. Any exemption must be explicit, scoped,
time-bound, owned, and reviewed before expiry.
#{{ENDIF}}

## Policy lifecycle

| State | Required evidence |
|---|---|
| Propose | Business requirement, affected scopes, test cases |
| Audit | Compliance impact and false-positive review |
| Remediate | Owner and due date for existing resources |
| Enforce | Clean pilot, exception record, rollback plan |
| Retire | Replacement control or accepted risk |

## Exception standard

Every exception must record:

- control and resource scope;
- business justification and compensating controls;
- accountable owner and approver;
- creation and expiration dates;
- remediation plan;
- evidence link.

Permanent undocumented exceptions are not permitted.

